Word embeddings: fifty numbers

rec_glove &lt;- recipe(label ~ text, data = train_data) |&gt;
  step_tokenize(text) |&gt;
  step_word_embeddings(text, embeddings = glove,
                       aggregation = "mean")    # mean per abstract

glove_wf &lt;- workflow() |&gt;
  add_recipe(rec_glove) |&gt;
  add_model(logit_spec)                         # Part III's model

m_glove &lt;- fit(glove_wf, data = train_data)

pred_glove &lt;- predict(m_glove, new_data = test_data) |&gt;
  bind_cols(test_data |&gt; select(id, label))


Sentence embeddings: 768 numbers

# the request from two slides ago, in batches of 32
emb &lt;- ollama_embed(science$text)               # 5,250 × 768
emb_tbl &lt;- as_tibble(emb,
                     .name_repair = ~ paste0("e", seq_along(.x))) |&gt;
  mutate(id = science$id)

train_emb &lt;- train_data |&gt; select(id, label) |&gt;
  left_join(emb_tbl, by = "id")
test_emb  &lt;- test_data |&gt; select(id, label) |&gt;
  left_join(emb_tbl, by = "id")

m_sent &lt;- fit(logit_spec, label ~ . - id,       # Part III's model
              data = train_emb)

pred_sent &lt;- predict(m_sent, test_emb) |&gt;
  bind_cols(test_emb |&gt; select(id, label))


The same logistic regression both times. Only what it reads has changed.


The two columns are the same move. On the left, a recipe step does the lookup, so the abstract stays text until the workflow reaches it. On the right, the embeddings are computed once, outside the workflow, because each one takes a call to a local model: that took about five minutes for 5,250 abstracts, so the deck loads the cached test-set predictions and computes the metrics from them. The code is what ran, in _scripts/prepare_week3_data.R. An abstract with a word the training set never contained still lands near abstracts that used its neighbours, on both sides.

01 · Obtain



Public data sets: parliamentary records, news corpora, research archives; ideal when they exist
Scraping: websites and archives you collect yourself; trivial or very hard depending on the site, and always a legal question
Third parties: APIs, commercial archives, data donations; custom formats, rate limits, terms of use that change



  Step 01
  From page to text
  what a scraper receives, and the part you keep
&lt;div class="story"&gt;
  &lt;p class="lede"&gt;North Korea launched a ballistic missile into the Sea of Japan, South Korea's military said.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;div class="ad"&gt;Advertisement&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;a href="/share"&gt;Share&lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;script src="tracker.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
  ↓
  North Korea launched a ballistic missile into the Sea of Japan, South Korea's military said.



Obtaining text is rarely the interesting part and often the slowest. Plan for it.


The card is one news sentence as a scraper receives it. The sentence is a small part of the page; the rest is markup, an advertisement label and a share link. Taking all the text on the page would keep Advertisement and Share as words in the corpus, so you select the element you want and drop the rest. Every source has its own version of this problem, which is why this step is slow.
